---
import PageLayout from '@layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE, LEGAL } from '@config/site';

const RUBRIC = [
  {
    criterion: 'Core capability',
    weight: '30%',
    detail:
      'Does the product perform its primary job under a documented workflow and test environment?',
  },
  {
    criterion: 'Ease of use',
    weight: '20%',
    detail: 'How long does a documented user take to reach a working workflow from a new account?',
  },
  {
    criterion: 'Integrations',
    weight: '20%',
    detail: 'How capable and reliable are the connectors relevant to the category and stated use case?',
  },
  {
    criterion: 'Value for money',
    weight: '20%',
    detail: 'What is the realistic cost at the stated volume, including required add-ons and usage limits?',
  },
  {
    criterion: 'Support',
    weight: '10%',
    detail: 'What can be observed from documented support interactions, response times and resolution quality?',
  },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="How We Test"
  description={`The evaluation framework ${SITE.name} requires for new and revised software scores.`}
  effectiveDate={LEGAL.effectiveDate}
  lastUpdated={LEGAL.lastUpdated}
  prose={false}
>
  <div class="sp-prose prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <p class="lead">
      This page is the evaluation framework for new and revised scored content. It is not proof
      that every historic page has completed every step. A score should only claim hands-on
      testing when the page can document the actual scope, environment and date.
    </p>

    <h2 id="principles">The three rules</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Document the plan and environment.</strong> A review should state whether it
        used a paid plan, trial, demo or another access route, and identify material limits.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Separate evaluation from monetization.</strong> Potential affiliate compensation
        must not decide a score, rank or editorial conclusion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Compare like with like.</strong> A comparison should use the same documented
        workflow and volume assumptions for every product being compared.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <section class="not-prose my-10" aria-labelledby="rubric-heading">
    <h2 id="rubric-heading" class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink-950">The scoring rubric</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-ink-600">
      Where StackPipeline publishes a score, this is the intended weighting. A page should
      explain any meaningful deviation from the rubric rather than imply a false precision.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-ink-200 shadow-card">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <caption class="sr-only">{SITE.name} scoring rubric and criterion weights</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-ink-950 text-white">
            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider">Criterion</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider">Weight</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider">What we measure</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-ink-100">
          {
            RUBRIC.map((row, index) => (
              <tr class={index % 2 === 1 ? 'bg-ink-50/50' : 'bg-white'}>
                <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-3.5 align-top font-semibold text-ink-900">{row.criterion}</th>
                <td class="px-4 py-3.5 align-top font-bold text-emerald-700">{row.weight}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3.5 align-top text-ink-700">{row.detail}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="sp-prose prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h2 id="workflow-testing">Workflow testing</h2>
    <p>
      For automation products, a credible test should publish enough detail for readers to
      understand the workflow: trigger, actions, source and destination systems, expected
      volume, failure handling, and any behavior that could change the result. Useful scenarios
      include lead routing, CRM enrichment, notifications, scheduled synchronization and a
      webhook flow with a deliberately failing downstream request.
    </p>

    <h2 id="data-enrichment-testing">Data enrichment testing</h2>
    <p>
      Match-rate or accuracy claims should identify the benchmark size, relevant geography or
      segment mix, sampling method, date and manual-validation method. We will not present a
      vendor-reported match rate as an independently observed result without saying so.
    </p>

    <h2 id="minimum-evidence">Minimum evidence for future scored content</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Full review</strong> — state the access level, testing period, hours or sessions, and limitations.</li>
      <li><strong>Comparison entry</strong> — apply the same named workflow and assumptions to every contender.</li>
      <li><strong>Integration tutorial</strong> — describe the integration steps, assumptions and known failure modes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="updates">Updates and limitations</h2>
    <p>
      Scores and price-sensitive claims should be rechecked after a material vendor change or a
      documented error. An update date should describe a real review event, not a cosmetic
      rewrite. No evaluation can cover every tier, region, volume or long-term reliability
      outcome; those limits should be named on the relevant page.
    </p>

    <h2 id="questions">Questions about a score</h2>
    <p>
      A verified public corrections channel has not yet been configured. Before scored content
      or advertising expands, StackPipeline will publish a monitored way to challenge an error
      and a process for recording substantive corrections.
    </p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
